  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nVery little change in the forecast over the next 24-48 hours as \nsurface and upper air pattern remains relatively stagnant thanks to \nbookend lows over New England and California. This leaves persistent \nmid/upper level ridging over the Great Lakes. These features do \nslowly drift eastward during this time with some modest return flow \nstarting later Tue but overall a lean toward persistence is \nwarranted, especially for Monday. This entails another night of good \nradiational cooling (though probably not quite as cold as last \nnight) followed by a large diurnal swing into Monday. Continued \nprevious trend of significantly reducing NBM dewpoints and enhancing \nthe diurnal temp ranges a bit. Some patchy fog possible again \ntonight but the signals in guidance are mixed and muted with another \nday of deep mixing/drying this afternoon so will hold off on \nmentioning fog in the forecast for now. With slowly increasing \nreturn flow and deep mixing Tue-Wed, highs could make a run at upper \n70s/around 80F.\n\nStill a fair degree of variability in numerical guidance heading \ninto the latter half of the week. Uncertainty centers around \nCalifornia closed low interacting with the remnants of Hurricane \nPolo. The speed of a northern stream upper jet traversing Canada is \nalso in question and the interaction of these features will dictate \nour sensible weather late-week. Earlier noted trend toward a slower \njet/trough and associated surface boundary continued in the 12Z \nsuite, suggesting precip may not start here until well into Thu. \nReasonable agreement on this within the ensemble guidance so NBM \nPoP's remain very low Wed into Wed night but do want to see more \ntemporal consistency before latching on entirely. There has also \nbeen a trend toward a more progressive frontal passage late Thu that \nmay clear our area entirely by Fri. This would alleviate any heavy \nrain concerns. Severe weather chances also seem very low given lack \nof strong midlevel forcing along the actual surface front and \noverall modest low level moisture/instability as we enter Oct. \nHowever, would still like to see more temporal consistency before \nlatching on to any one scenario. Still plenty of details to work out \nfor late-week but overall the risk of strong storms/heavy rain does \nappear low.",
